Do not reverse this order by starting with:

* autonomous containment
* real process termination
* external network actions
* unrestricted AI tools
* production honeypots
* credential collection
* kernel-level monitoring

25. Definition of Done

A feature is complete only when:

* implementation exists
* unit tests exist
* integration tests exist where applicable
* failure behavior is defined
* security impact is documented
* logs and audit records are present
* configuration is documented
* dry-run behavior is preserved
* no secrets are introduced
* CI passes
* documentation is updated
* the related GitHub issue is closed through the pull request

26. Final GitHub Workflow

The complete development workflow should look like this:

Write specification
      ↓
Split specification into documentation and issues
      ↓
Create milestone
      ↓
Create feature branch
      ↓
Implement one bounded capability
      ↓
Add tests
      ↓
Run linting, type checks, and security checks
      ↓
Open pull request
      ↓
Review architecture and security impact
      ↓
Merge into develop
      ↓
Run integration tests
      ↓
Create release pull request
      ↓
Tag release
      ↓
Publish changelog
      ↓
Deploy only after authorization and security review

The most important implementation rule is:

AI proposes.
Schema validation filters.
Policy decides.
Authorization approves.
Least-privileged components execute.
Audit logging records everything.

BackSpin should first become a reliable, testable, dry-run defensive pipeline. Real containment, real deception infrastructure, and production deployment should be added only after the event model, policy boundaries, authorization checks, and security tests are stable.